---
const approaches = [
  {
    name: 'Hand-written config',
    how: 'Manual includeIf "gitdir:…" blocks in ~/.gitconfig and Host aliases in ~/.ssh/config.',
    ide: 'Native (Git & OpenSSH)',
    tradeoff: 'Full control with nothing to install, but every new key, folder, or alias is manual and mistakes are silent.',
    isGitsetu: false
  },
  {
    name: 'Switch on demand',
    how: 'CLI rewrites your global Git identity or active SSH key whenever you run a switch command.',
    ide: 'Follows last command',
    tradeoff: 'Simple mental model, but the active identity follows your last command rather than the repo you are in.',
    isGitsetu: false
  },
  {
    name: 'Shell hooks / direnv',
    how: 'Exports GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL or GIT_SSH_COMMAND dynamically whenever your shell runs cd.',
    ide: 'Requires IDE plugins',
    tradeoff: 'General purpose for shell workflows, but GUI clients and IDEs outside the shell hook need separate wiring.',
    isGitsetu: false
  },
  {
    name: 'Generated native config (GitSetu)',
    how: 'Compiles directory & remote-URL includeIf routes, per-profile Git files, and an OpenSSH Include once, then exits.',
    ide: 'Native everywhere (IDEs & GUIs)',
    tradeoff: 'Git and OpenSSH handle runtime routing with 0 KB daemon overhead; follows Git’s and OpenSSH’s own precedence rules.',
    isGitsetu: true
  }
];
---

<section class="comparison section reveal" id="compare">
  <div id="comparison" style="position: absolute; top: -80px;"></div>
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title">How GitSetu <span class="accent-text">compares.</span></h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">An honest look at architectural approaches and trade-offs — without guessing other tools' feature checklists.</p>

    <div class="table-container" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Git identity management approaches comparison">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="approach-col">Approach</th>
            <th scope="col">How it works</th>
            <th scope="col">IDE &amp; GUI Clients</th>
            <th scope="col">Trade-off</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {approaches.map((row) => (
            <tr class={row.isGitsetu ? 'gitsetu-row' : ''}>
              <th scope="row" class="approach-cell">
                <span class={row.isGitsetu ? 'gitsetu-badge' : ''}>{row.name}</span>
              </th>
              <td>{row.how}</td>
              <td class="ide-cell">
                <span class={row.isGitsetu ? 'pill-accent' : 'pill-muted'}>{row.ide}</span>
              </td>
              <td>{row.tradeoff}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="boundaries-grid">
      <div class="boundary-card card-fits">
        <div class="boundary-header">
          <span class="boundary-dot dot-success" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Where GitSetu Fits Best</h3>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Multi-account workspaces:</strong> Automatic switching by directory (<code>gitdir</code>) or remote URL (<code>--remote</code>) across CLI, IDEs, and GUI clients.</li>
          <li><strong>Clean coexistence:</strong> Writes only marked blocks and isolated files, preserving manual configs, signing settings, and project hooks.</li>
          <li><strong>Verifiable ops:</strong> Built-in <code>explain</code>, <code>audit</code>, <code>doctor --repair</code>, opt-in pre-commit guard, and authenticated v2 vaults.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="boundary-card card-alt">
        <div class="boundary-header">
          <span class="boundary-dot dot-warn" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>When Another Approach Fits Better</h3>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Single identity:</strong> One account or two that never share a machine? Plain hand-written <code>includeIf</code> blocks are plenty.</li>
          <li><strong>Server-side enforcement:</strong> GitSetu’s guard runs client-side; use your Git host’s push rules if you need unbypassable policy enforcement.</li>
          <li><strong>Agent-only SSH keys:</strong> If keys live only inside 1Password or Secretive, note that external SSH agent support is planned for <strong>v1.2.0</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="compare-more">
      <a href="/docs/overview/comparisons" class="compare-link">
        <span>Read the full comparison &amp; links to related tools</span>
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line><polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

